course: dictionary
lesson: 373
title: Words about how people behave
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Antisocial describes behaviour that does not show care for other people. Break the word into parts: anti means against, and social means to do with people living together. So antisocial means against the people around you. «The police can give young people a warning for antisocial behaviour, and parents may have to pay for damage.» The clues are warning, damage and pay: the behaviour harms other people, so it is wrong.
explain: The word can also describe someone who does not want company. A person who keeps to themselves and does not like being with others can be called antisocial. Read the words around it to decide which meaning fits. Harm or damage points to the first meaning; keeping away from others points to the second.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence and seeing if it still makes sense. Antisocial is stronger than unfriendly, though. It describes behaviour that hurts or annoys the whole community, not just a bad mood.
fact: Antisocial is an adjective, so it describes a noun: antisocial behaviour, or an antisocial neighbour.
fact: Its other forms are more antisocial and most antisocial.
fact: «Antisocial» can mean behaving in a way that harms or annoys other people, like vandalism or shouting late at night.
fact: «Antisocial» can also mean not wanting to be with other people, or being shy of company.
fact: Antisocial hours are working hours late at night, when most people are asleep.
exercise: «The police can give young people a warning for antisocial behaviour.» What does antisocial behaviour mean here? | Behaviour that is very quiet | Behaviour that makes people laugh together | Behaviour that happens only at the seaside | *Behaviour that harms or annoys other people
exercise: Mia spends every break alone with a book and does not like company. Which word describes her best? | famished | *antisocial | ancient | luminous
exercise: Dad works antisocial hours. What does this phrase mean? | Hours spent at the beach | *Working hours late at night | Hours of school lessons | Hours spent shopping in town
test: Graffiti on a bus shelter and litter dropped on the pavement are examples of ___ behaviour. | famished | ancient | *antisocial | luminous
test: «The neighbours complained about the antisocial noise from the party at 3 a.m.» What does this mean? | The party was very bright | A quiet, peaceful night | A very old party | *Behaviour that disturbs other people
exam: Which definition fits someone who keeps to themselves and does not want company? | Working very late at night | *Not wanting to be with other people | Very old and wise | Doing damage to property
